  "architectural_summary": "**Architectural Summary: PocketBase**\n\n**1. Overall Architecture Pattern**\nThis is a monolithic, self-contained backend application built in Go, designed as a single executable with embedded SQLite. The structure follows a layered architecture with clear separation between API handlers (`apis/`), data access objects (`daos/`), business logic/forms (`forms/`), and core models (`models/`).\n\n**2. Tech Stack**\n- **Backend Framework**: Echo v5 (via `github.com/labstack/echo/v5`) for HTTP routing and middleware.\n- **Database**: SQLite (via `mattn/go-sqlite3` and `modernc.org/sqlite`), with migrations managed in `migrations/`. The `dbx` library (`github.com/pocketbase/dbx`) provides database abstraction.\n- **Authentication**: JWT (`golang.org/x/crypto`, `github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v4`) with OAuth2 support (`golang.org/x/oauth2`).\n- **File Storage**: Local filesystem and S3 integration via `gocloud.dev` and AWS SDK.\n- **Email**: SMTP and sendmail via `tools/mailer/`.\n- **Validation**: Ozzo-validation (`github.com/go-ozzo/ozzo-validation/v4`) and govalidator.\n\n**3. API Surface**\nRESTful API with endpoints for admin, collections, records, files, logs, settings, and realtime subscriptions (see `apis/`). WebSocket support for realtime updates (`apis/realtime.go`). The API is structured around resources like admins, users, collections, and records, with comprehensive CRUD operations.\n\n**4. Data Layer**\n- **ORM/Query Builder**: Custom DAO layer (`daos/`) built on `dbx`.\n- **Key Models**: `Admin`, `User`, `Collection`, `Record`, `Param`, `Request`, and `Log` (in `models/`). Collections define schemas (`models/schema/`) for dynamic record structures, enabling a flexible, database-like backend.\n- **Relationships**: Collections contain records; users/admins have authentication tokens; logs track system activity.\n\n**5. Infrastructure Maturity**\n- **CI/CD**: GitHub Actions (indicated by `ci_cd: \"github-actions\"`).\n- **Containerization**: Not explicitly containerized (no Dockerfile in provided tree).\n- **Deployment**: Single-binary deployment with embedded SQLite, suitable for standalone or lightweight containerized deployments.\n\n**6. Strengths**\n- **Clean Separation**: Well-organized layers (API, DAO, forms, models) with extensive unit testing (most files have `_test.go` counterparts).\n- **Extensibility**: Hook system (`tools/hook/`), OAuth2 providers (`tools/auth/`), and pluggable file storage.\n- **Self-Contained**: Minimal external dependencies; includes migrations, email templates, and admin UI (implied by `mails/templates/` and admin APIs).\n\n**7. Weaknesses / Gaps**\n- **Database Scalability**: Reliance on embedded SQLite limits horizontal scaling and high-write workloads.\n- **Infrastructure Gaps**: No built-in caching, message queues, or cloud-native deployment artifacts (e.g., Dockerfile, Kubernetes manifests).\n- **Monolithic Constraints**: While well-structured, the monolith may become cumbersome for microservices-oriented environments. Real-time features (`apis/realtime.go`) may face scalability challenges without dedicated WebSocket infrastructure.\n\n**Overall**: PocketBase is a robust, batteries-included backend for small to medium applications, emphasizing simplicity and rapid development. Its architecture is clean and testable but optimized for vertical scaling and standalone use.",
